WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
A splash of fresh water will reach the depths of your heart. Those mighty blue waves can propel you forward or they can calm and soothe. Coast to Michigan coast. Simply feel the flow in the moment. And let those vast lakes, rivers and streams create everlasting memories. Jump right in and let fresh wash over you in Pure Michigan. Keep it fresh at Michigan.org. Or on our website.
清新的水花将触及你的心灵深处。那些浩荡的蓝色波浪可以推动你向前，也能带来平静与慰藉。从密歇根的这头到那头。只需感受当下流淌的思绪。让那些广袤的湖泊、河流和溪水创造永恒的记忆。纵身跃入，让清泉在纯净的密歇根涤荡你的身心。请访问 Michigan.org 保持新鲜感，或浏览我们的网站。

2
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Valda Thomas didn't sleep well on the night of Tuesday, April 25, 1978. That evening, her husband Bill and his brother Rupert had returned from a day of foraging for mushrooms in the bush near Churro, a small South Australian township 90 kilometres northeast of the city of Adelaide. During their hunt, they had found something that Valda couldn't stop thinking about. Partially buried under some light scrub was what appeared to be the leg bone of a cow. Although the brothers noted it as strange, they carried on mushrooming without giving it too much thought. When Valda heard about the bone, she was rattled. The bushland surrounding Churro was dry and brittle and definitely wasn't suitable for cattle grazing.
1978年4月25日星期二晚上，瓦尔达·托马斯（Valda Thomas）没睡好觉。那天晚上，她的丈夫比尔（Bill）和他的兄弟鲁珀特（Rupert）从南澳大利亚州小镇楚罗（Truro）附近的灌木丛中 foraging（搜寻）蘑菇回来，楚罗位于阿德莱德市东北方90公里处。在搜寻过程中，他们发现了一些让瓦尔达挥之不去的东西。浅浅的灌木丛下半掩埋着疑似牛腿骨的东西。虽然兄弟俩觉得这很奇怪，但他们并没有多想，继续采蘑菇。当瓦尔达听到关于这块骨头的事时，她感到一阵恐慌。楚罗周边的灌木林干燥易碎，绝对不适合牛群放牧。

3
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:38.000
It was an inhospitable area for anything other than rabbits and native wildlife and Valda couldn't recall ever seeing cows during her previous visits out there. She went to bed thinking about the bone and when she finally drifted off into a restless sleep, a voice came to her in her dreams. It beckoned her to return to Churro, saying, come, come. Two days later, Valda urged Bill to take her to the spot where the bone was found. The husband and wife made the drive down the Sturt Highway,
除了兔子和本地野生动物之外，这是一个不适合任何生物生存的区域，瓦尔达记不清自己以前去那里时见过牛。她带着对这块骨头的想法上床睡觉，当她终于陷入不安的睡眠时，一个声音在她的梦中响起。它召唤她回到楚罗，说，来吧，来吧。两天后，瓦尔达敦促比尔带她去发现骨头的地方。这对夫妇沿着斯图尔特公路（Sturt Highway）驱车前行，

4
00:02:38.000 --> 00:03:37.000
past rolling acres of agricultural land and sweeping plains until they reached Churro. With the population of only 800 residents, the wheat farming town didn't offer much for visitors. Passes by had little reason to stop in the area unless they needed a break between travelling to other destinations. Valda and Bill ventured onwards for 16 kilometers, eventually pulling off the highway and onto Swamp Road, a dirt stretch that led to rugged flood plains scattered with mally trees and native scrubs. They parked the car and Bill attempted to retrace the steps that he and Rupert had taken during their hunt for mushrooms. Finally, he found the spot. The leg bone was still there, partially hidden under some brittle branches.
驶过起伏的农田和广袤的平原，直到他们到达楚罗。这个小麦耕作小镇仅有800名居民，并没有给游客提供太多东西。路过的人几乎没有理由在这个地区停留，除非他们需要在前往其他目的地的旅途中休息一下。瓦尔达和比尔继续前行了16公里，最终驶离公路，拐上沼泽路（Swamp Road），这是一条通往点缀着马利树和本土灌木的崎岖洪泛区的泥土路。他们把车停下，比尔试图沿着他和鲁珀特寻找蘑菇时走过的足迹往回找。终于，找到了那个地方。那根腿骨还在，半掩在枯脆的树枝下。

5
00:03:37.000 --> 00:04:47.000
This time, Bill and Valda drew closer to inspect it. They pulled it up, and to their horror, they discovered that on the other end of the bone was a woman's shoe. Inside were the remains of her painted toenails. The next day 18-year-old Veronica Knight had been looking forward to Christmas of 1976 for months. Not only did she enjoy the festive season, but this year was particularly special. Veronica had plans to visit some close friends in Melbourne who had recently welcomed their first baby. The trip was a big deal for Veronica.
这一次，比尔和瓦尔达走近去检查它。他们把它拉了起来，惊恐地发现骨头的另一端是一只女人的鞋。里面还残留着涂了指甲油的脚趾甲。第二天，18岁的维罗妮卡·奈特（Veronica Knight）已经盼望1976年的圣诞节好几个月了。她不仅喜欢节日季，而且今年的意义尤为特殊。维罗妮卡计划去墨尔本探望一些刚刚迎来第一个孩子的亲密朋友。这次旅行对维罗妮卡来说是一件大事。

6
00:04:47.000 --> 00:05:48.000
Having been raised in the foster care system and spent most of her life in institutions, she didn't have any close family. Her mother passed away when she was just a baby, and her father was an alcoholic who felt unable to raise her on his own. It wasn't until Veronica was a teenager that she really began to feel part of the community. She moved into a boarding home for girls and became involved with a local Anglican church. It was there that she formed a close bond with a married couple named Peter and Jeanette Woods. Veronica spent many happy weekends with the Woods, staying at their house, helping with church duties, and baking in the kitchen with Jeanette. When the Woods announced they were relocating to Melbourne to have their first child, Veronica was crushed, but she also looked forward to being able to visit them in the big city.
由于在寄养系统中长大，大半生都在机构度过，她没有任何亲近的家人。她的母亲在她还是个婴儿时就去世了，父亲是个酗酒者，觉得自己无力独自抚养她。直到维罗妮卡十几岁时，她才真正开始感觉到自己是社区的一部分。她搬进了一家女孩寄宿家庭，并参与了当地的一所圣公会教堂。正是在那里，她与一对名叫彼得（Peter）和珍妮特·伍兹（Jeanette Woods）的夫妇建立了深厚的感情。维罗妮卡和伍兹夫妇度过了许多快乐的周末，住在他们家里，帮忙做教堂的工作，并和珍妮特一起在厨房里烘焙。当伍兹夫妇宣布他们要搬到墨尔本迎他们的第一个孩子时，维罗妮卡心碎了，但她也很期待能够去大城市看望他们。

7
00:05:48.000 --> 00:06:45.000
She spent the next few months working part-time to save enough money to facilitate the journey. By December of 1976, Veronica had everything perfectly planned out. Her train ticket from Adelaide was booked for the evening of December 26, and the overnight journey would see her arriving in Melbourne the morning after. She would spend a week with Peter and Jeanette before returning to Adelaide on New Year's Eve. Veronica had a few purchases she needed to make first, so on the evening of December 23, she and her friend Jenny made their way into Rundlemore, a pedestrian shopping strip in the heart of Adelaide's CBD. The city was bustling with last-minute Christmas shoppers, and the girls browsed happily, soaking up the festive atmosphere.
在接下来的几个月里，她打零工攒够了钱来支付这次旅行的费用。到1976年12月，维罗妮卡已经把一切都完美地计划好了。她从阿德莱德出发的火车票预订在12月26日晚，经过一夜的行程，她将在第二天早晨抵达墨尔本。她将在彼得和珍妮特那里度过一周，然后在除夕夜返回阿德莱德。维罗妮卡有一些东西需要先买，所以在12月23日晚上，她和朋友珍妮（Jenny）来到了阿德莱德中央商务区中心的步行购物街朗德莫尔（Rundle Mall）。城市里挤满了最后关头采购圣诞节礼物的购物者，两个女孩开心地逛着，沉浸在节日的气氛中。

8
00:06:45.000 --> 00:07:44.000
Veronica bought some new clothes and a present for the Woods' baby. After a few hours, Jenny noticed it was getting late. The girls had recently moved into a Salvation Army boarding house, which enforced a strict curfew of 11.30pm. They decided they'd better head to the bus stop to avoid getting in trouble. Along the way, they passed through the city cross-arcade and made a last-minute decision to have some photos taken in a black-and-white photo booth. Afterwards, they were almost at their bus stop on King William Road, one of the city's main thoroughfares, when Veronica stopped short. She realized she'd left one of her shopping bags in the photo booth. She told Jenny to go ahead without her and that she'd run back to grab her bags, then catch the next bus home.
维罗妮卡买了一些新衣服和送给伍兹夫妇孩子的礼物。几个小时后，珍妮注意到时间不早了。女孩们最近搬进了一家救世军寄宿公寓，那里实行晚上11点半的严格宵禁。她们决定最好去公交车站，以免惹上麻烦。一路上，她们穿过了城市十字长廊（city cross-arcade），并临时决定在一个黑白照相亭里拍几张照片。之后，她们快走到位于城市主要干道之一威廉国王路（King William Road）的公交车站时，维罗妮卡突然停下脚步。她意识到自己把其中一个购物袋忘在照相亭里了。她告诉珍妮先走，她要跑回去拿袋子，然后坐下一班车回家。

9
00:07:44.000 --> 00:08:49.000
When Jenny arrived at the boarding house without Veronica, the house matron was immediately concerned. She waited up to make sure that Veronica got home safely, but as the minutes ticked by, there was no sign of her. By 1am, the matrons' worries peaked. She found Veronica's address book and phoned some of her friends to check if Veronica had perhaps stayed at one of their homes, but nobody had seen or heard from her. The next day, the matron filed a missing person report. The police didn't seem overly concerned. It was an unusual for young women to run away or go off with friends without telling anyone before reappearing unharmed a few days later. Those close to Veronica knew how excited she was about her trip to Melbourne and hoped that she'd simply show up as planned.
当珍妮没有维罗妮卡陪同抵达寄宿公寓时，宿管阿姨立刻担心起来。她熬夜等候以确保维罗妮卡安全到家，但随着分秒流逝，依然不见她的踪影。到了凌晨1点，宿管的担忧达到了顶点。她找到了维罗妮卡的通讯录，给她的几个朋友打电话，想确认维罗妮卡是否住在他们家，但没有人见过或听说过她。第二天，宿管报了人口失踪。警方似乎并不太担心。年轻女性离家出走或在不通知任何人的情况下与朋友外出，几天后又安然无恙地出现，这并不是什么稀奇事。那些与维罗妮卡亲近的人知道她对墨尔本之行有多兴奋，并希望她能像计划的那样出现。

10
00:08:49.000 --> 00:10:00.000
On the morning of December 27, 1976, Peter Woods stood on the platform of Melbourne's Spencer Street station, waiting for the overland train from Adelaide to arrive. As he watched the passengers disembark, it became clear that Veronica wasn't among them. He called his wife Jeanette from a payphone and said, She didn't come. By the time Vowder and Bill Thomas discovered the human leg bone in the bushland surrounding Truro, Veronica Knight had been missing for 16 months. Given the opinion of police that she had run away, there hadn't been any searches for her, nor had her disappearance received any publicity. Police descended on the Truro area and searched a 600 meter radius from where the leg bone was found. They recovered the scattered remains of a young woman, along with a pair of jeans and a green cardigan.
1976年12月27日早晨，彼得·伍兹站在墨尔本斯潘塞街车站（Spencer Street station）的站台上，等待从阿德莱德开来的长途火车抵达。当他看着乘客下车时，很明显维罗妮卡并不在其中。他用公用电话打给妻子珍妮特说，她没有来。当瓦尔达和比尔·托马斯在特鲁罗（Truro）周边的灌木丛中发现人类腿骨时，维罗妮卡·奈特已经失踪16个月了。鉴于警方认为她离家出走的观点，没有任何人对她进行过搜索，她的失踪也没有受到任何公开报道。警方大举搜查了特鲁罗地区，并在发现腿骨方圆600米的范围内进行了搜寻。他们找回了一名年轻女性分散的遗骸，以及一条牛仔裤和一件绿色开衫。

11
00:10:00.000 --> 00:11:24.000
It was Veronica Knight. Due to the skeletal state of Veronica's remains, there was nothing to indicate the cause of her death. The location near Swamp Road led investigators to deduce that Veronica had likely wandered into the bush in the summer heat without adequate supplies, where she became disoriented and died of dehydration. However, this explanation didn't entirely add up. Veronica had absolutely no reason to be in such an isolated place, and it seemed highly unlikely that she would have ventured so far given her upcoming trip to Melbourne she had been so looking forward to. Furthermore, the area of Adelaide where she was last seen was over an hour's drive from Truro, and she had no means of transport to get herself there. When Veronica went missing, she had been carrying a black purse with approximately $180 cash in it, a significant amount of money at the time, but the purse was nowhere to be seen. If she had simply gotten lost following a bush walk gone awry, surely the purse would still have been with her.
那是维罗妮卡·奈特。由于维罗妮卡的遗骸呈骨骼状态，没有任何迹象表明她的死因。沼泽路（Swamp Road）附近的位置让调查人员推断，维罗妮卡很可能在夏日的炎热中、在没有充足补给的情况下误入灌木丛，在那里迷失方向并脱水身亡。然而，这种解释并不能完全说得通。维罗妮卡绝对没有理由去这样一个偏僻的地方，鉴于她一直翘首以盼的即将到来的墨尔本之行，她冒险走得这么远似乎极不可能。此外，她最后一次被看到的阿德莱德地区距离特鲁罗有一个多小时的车程，而且她没有交通工具去那里。当维罗妮卡失踪时，她随身携带一个黑色钱包，里面大约有180现金，在当时是一笔相当可观的钱，但钱包却不知所踪。如果她只是在一次偏离路线的灌木丛散步后迷路了，钱包肯定还在她身上。

12
00:11:24.000 --> 00:12:42.000
Investigators admitted that Veronica's death was suspicious, but with no evidence to suggest an alternative and no close family to fight for answers, her case was shelved. However, there was one investigator who wasn't prepared to accept that Veronica's death was a tragic accident. Detective Sergeant Bob Giles had been working in the missing persons division of the South Australian Police, and had noticed a strange pattern emerge. During the seven-week timeframe between Christmas of 1976 and early February of 1977, three young women had gone missing from the city centre after waiting for a bus. Four others had gone missing from the vicinity of a bus stop or main road on the city's outskirts. No witnesses had seen anything untoward, and each woman had therefore been marked by the police as voluntarily absent. Partly because of this, news of their disappearances had never been made public. Detective Giles wasn't buying it.
调查人员承认维罗妮卡的死因可疑，但由于没有证据表明有其他可能，也没有亲近的家人为寻求答案而奔走，她的案件被搁置了。然而，有一位调查人员并不准备接受维罗妮卡的死是一场悲惨事故的说法。侦探警长鲍勃·吉尔斯（Bob Giles）一直在南澳大利亚警察局的失踪人口科工作，并注意到了一种奇怪的模式出现。在1976年圣诞节到1977年2月初之间的七周时间里，有三名年轻女性在等完公交车后从市中心失踪。另有四人从城市郊区的公交车站或主要道路附近失踪。没有目击者看到任何异常情况，因此每位女性都被警方登记为自行离去。部分由于这一点，她们失踪的消息从未公之于众。吉尔斯侦探并不买账。

13
00:12:42.000 --> 00:13:41.000
Although none of the missing women were personally connected in any way, he believed there were simply too many similarities between each case for the disappearances to be coincidental. These weren't vulnerable women living on the outskirts of society. Each missing woman came from good homes and had loved ones who were expecting them. They had all disappeared without making further contact or collecting any of their belongings. Although some of them had run off in the past, to Detective Giles, they didn't seem like typical runaways. His theory was far more serious. He believed that a serial killer was at play. South Australia in the 1970s was considered a relatively safe place.
尽管失踪的女性之间没有任何个人联系，但他认为每个案件之间的相似之处实在太多，不可能是巧合。这些并不是生活在社会边缘的弱势女性。每个失踪的女性都来自良好的家庭，并且都有爱她们、期盼她们归来的人。她们都在没有进一步联系或拿走任何个人物品的情况下消失了。尽管其中一些人过去曾离家出走过，但在吉尔斯侦探看来，她们并不像典型的离家出走者。他的理论要严重得多。他认为有一个连环杀手在作案。20世纪70年代的南澳大利亚被认为是一个相对安全的地方。

14
00:13:41.000 --> 00:15:01.000
The most notorious crimes related to children, including the mysterious disappearance of siblings Jane, Anna and Grant Beaumont as covered in episode 100 of Casefile, and the suspected abduction of Joanne Ratcliffe and Kirsty Gordon as covered in episode 163 of Casefile. Serial killings were unheard of, and most murders were committed by someone known to the victim. Therefore, Detective Giles's theory that a serial killer had been operating in Adelaide during the summer of 76 and 77 was extreme. Nonetheless, he presented his findings to the officer in charge of the major crime squad, who was flawed by the revelation. He agreed that the seven disappearances were likely the cause of a serial offender and immediately appointed an investigative team to launch a full inquiry. The investigative team knew the only way to bring attention to the pattern of missing women was for one of their loved ones to speak out. If they could raise public awareness, someone might come forward with information that could lead to their whereabouts.
最臭名昭著的罪行与儿童有关，包括简、安娜和格兰特·博蒙特兄妹的神秘失踪（如《罪案实录》第100集所述），以及乔安妮·拉特克利夫和柯丝蒂·戈登的疑似绑架案（如《罪案实录》第163集所述）。连环杀人案闻所未闻，大多数谋杀案都是由受害者认识的人犯下的。因此，吉尔斯侦探关于连环杀手在76年和77年夏季在阿德莱德作案的理论是极其激进的。尽管如此，他还是将调查结果呈报给了重案组负责人，后者被这一发现震惊了。他同意这七起失踪事件很可能是由一名连环罪犯造成的，并立即指派了一个调查小组展开全面调查。调查小组知道，要让人们关注失踪女性的模式，唯一的办法就是让她们的至亲大声发声。如果他们能提高公众意识，可能会有人提供能解开她们下落的信息。

15
00:15:01.000 --> 00:16:23.000
However, many parents of the missing women were reluctant to speak publicly about their daughters. Some families wanted to avoid potential criticism, while others viewed their child's disappearance as a private issue. Detective Giles approached one of the only families he thought might be willing to help. 16-year-old Julie Makita was a trustworthy and sensible teenager, so when she phoned her parents at 9.30pm on the evening of January 21, 1977 and asked if she could stay out a little later, they didn't hesitate to give their permission. Julie's father told her to be home by 11, and she happily agreed. It was the summer holidays, and she was enjoying the youthful sense of freedom. Julie and her best friend Camilla had spent the day selling jewellery at a small street side stand in the city, each earning $10 for their efforts. When they closed up shop for the day, Camilla went home, while Julie and an acquaintance named Max returned the unsolved inventory to its owner in the Adelaide Central Market.
然而，许多失踪女性的父母都不愿公开谈论他们的女儿。一些家庭希望避免潜在的批评，而另一些家庭则将孩子的失踪视为私事。吉尔斯侦探找到了他认为可能愿意帮忙的唯一家庭之一。16岁的朱莉·马基塔（Julie Makita）是一个值得信赖且懂事的青少年，因此当她在1977年1月21日晚上9点30分给父母打电话询问是否可以晚点回家时，他们毫不犹豫地同意了。朱莉的父亲告诉她11点前回家，她高兴地答应了。那是暑假，她正在享受那种青春期的自由感。朱莉和她最好的朋友卡米拉（Camilla）白天在城市的一个小型街边摊位上卖珠宝，每人赚了10美元。当她们结束一天的生意时，卡米拉回家了，而朱莉和一个名叫马克斯（Max）的熟人将未卖出的库存归还给了阿德莱德中央市场的业主。

16
00:16:23.000 --> 00:17:26.000
Julie and Max then went to the Majestic Hotel on King William Street to play a few rounds of pool. The pub was right near the bus stop that would take her home to the inner eastern suburb of St. Peter's. 11pm came and went, and Julie didn't return home. Her parents, Irush and Anne-Marie, hoped she had simply stayed at a friend's house and forgotten to call, but when she still didn't make contact the next day, they became worried sick. They tracked Max down to see if he knew where Julie could be. Max said that after playing pool, he walked Julie to her bus stop on King William Street at around 10.30pm. As he walked away, he saw a white car pull up to the curb and call out to Julie. Julie approached the vehicle as though she knew the occupants.
朱莉和马克斯随后去了威廉国王街的雄伟酒店（Majestic Hotel）打了几局台球。这家酒吧就在公交车站附近，可以带她回内东郊的圣彼得斯（St. Peter's）。晚上11点到了又过去，朱莉没有回家。她的父母伊鲁什（Irush）和安娜-玛丽（Anne-Marie）希望她只是住在朋友家里，忘了打电话，但第二天她依然没有联系他们，这让他们急得像热锅上的蚂蚁。他们找到了马克斯，看他是否知道朱莉可能在哪里。马克斯说，打完台球后，他在晚上10点半左右把朱莉送到了威廉国王街的公交车站。当他走开时，他看到一辆白色的汽车停在路边，并向朱莉喊话。朱莉像认识车上的人一样向车走去。

17
00:17:26.000 --> 00:18:30.000
A man got out of the car, and Julie climbed into the middle of the front seat. The Makitas reported Julie's disappearance to the police, who mostly brushed it off as typical teenage antics, and assured the worried parents that their daughter would likely show up soon. Irush and Anne-Marie were convinced that Julie wasn't the kind of teenager who would run off with friends without letting them know. She was a good daughter and a student who planned on becoming a doctor. Maybe she had gotten into an accident or been injured and was unable to get in touch. Still, they had their doubts. Desperate for answers, Anne-Marie turned to Julie's journal and some letters she had written to her older sister who lived interstate. She discovered that perhaps she didn't know her daughter as well as she thought.
一个男人从车里出来，朱莉爬上了前排座椅中间。马基塔夫妇向警方报案朱莉失踪，警方大多将其斥为典型的青少年恶作剧，并向焦虑的父母保证女儿很可能会很快出现。伊鲁什和安娜-玛丽坚信朱莉不是那种不打招呼就和朋友跑掉的青少年。她是一个好女儿，是一个立志成为医生的学生。也许她出了事故或受伤了，无法取得联系。尽管如此，他们还是心存疑虑。渴望得到答案的安娜-玛丽翻看了朱莉的日记以及她写给她住在外州的姐姐的一些信件。她发现，或许她并不像自己想象的那样了解女儿。

18
00:18:30.000 --> 00:19:22.000
Julie wrote of partaking in risky behavior, such as hitchhiking. Anne-Marie was shocked. She questioned whether Julie had perhaps made the ill-informed decision to take off on an adventure after all, or perhaps join a commune. In hindsight, the last conversation Anne-Marie had with her daughter had been a strange one. Julie had asked about her mother's younger years and then said, We were very happy when we were children, my sister and I. Thank you for my childhood, mother. I just wanted you to know that. Anne-Marie wasn't sure whether to interpret that as Julie's subtle way of saying goodbye.
朱莉在日记中写到参与搭便车等危险行为。安娜-玛丽大吃一惊。她质疑朱莉是否最终做出了不明智的决定，去进行一场冒险，或者加入一个公社。事后回想起来，安娜-玛丽与女儿的最后一次对话是一个奇怪的对话。朱莉问起母亲年轻时的岁月，然后说，我们做孩子的时候非常快乐，我和我姐姐。谢谢你给我一个童年，妈妈。我只是想让你知道这一点。安娜-玛丽不确定是否应该将其解读为朱莉委婉的告别方式。

19
00:19:22.000 --> 00:21:13.000
Adding to her doubts was a poem she had found that Julie had written. It read, The day is done. All that could pass, past. All that could live, lived. All that is, was. All that was now, is then. The day is done. Julie had been missing for nearly two years when Detective Giles approached the Makita family with his theory that Julie and several other young women had fallen victim to foul play at the hands of a serial offender. Anne-Marie didn't know what to think. She had tried her hardest not to let her mind go to a dark place and now she was being forced to confront her worst fears head on. She and Irush agreed to front the media to raise awareness about the pattern of missing women in the hopes that would encourage someone with information to come forward. If nothing else, they hoped that sharing their story might persuade other teenage girls to be more careful when accepting rides from strangers. Anne-Marie said that Julie was a fastidious child. If someone had offered her a lift and tried to force himself on her, she would have fought back. Perhaps that had led the offender to kill her out of panic and then hide her body somewhere. Irush said the hardest thing about Julie's disappearance was not knowing the truth. He likened it to being on an emotional seesaw that was tearing the family apart.
加深她疑虑的是她发现的一首朱莉写的诗。上面写着，白昼已逝。一切能过去的，都过去了。一切能活过的，都活过了。一切存在的，都曾存在。一切曾是的，如今皆是。白昼已逝。当吉尔斯侦探带着他的理论接近马基塔家族时，朱莉已经失踪近两年了，他认为朱莉和其他几位年轻女性落入了连环罪犯的毒手。安娜-玛丽不知该作何感想。她曾竭尽全力不让自己的思绪陷入黑暗，而现在她被迫直面自己最恐惧的噩梦。她和伊鲁什同意出面面对媒体，以提高人们对失踪女性模式的认识，希望这能鼓励知情者挺身而出。退一步讲，他们希望分享他们的故事能够劝说其他少女在接受陌生人搭车时更加小心。安娜-玛丽说，朱莉是一个严谨的孩子。如果有人主动提供搭便车并试图对她施暴，她一定会反抗。也许这导致罪犯出于惊慌而杀害了她，然后把她的尸体藏在某个地方。伊鲁什说，朱莉失踪最痛苦的是不知道真相。他将其比作坐在一个正在撕裂这个家庭的情感跷跷板上。

20
00:21:13.000 --> 00:22:59.000
The Makita's story made local headlines, with reporters drawing a connection to other young women who had gone missing around the same time. But nobody came forward with information that shed light on any of the girls' whereabouts. A few weeks later, on April 15, 1979, a police officer was patrolling the Sturt Highway on his motorcycle. It was the Easter Long Weekend, a notoriously dangerous time on the northern roads as motorists flocked from the city to holiday on the nearby York Peninsula and in the Barossa Valley wine region. The officer was on the lookout for any reckless behavior when he was suddenly flagged down by a group of four young men. They'd been out hunting rabbits in the bush near Truro and had found something disturbing. They handed the item to the police officer wrapped in a sheet of plastic. It was a human skull. The youths directed police to the paddock where they had made the discovery. It was down Swamp Road, just 50 meters from the fence line. For investigators, this location immediately raised red flags. The location was less than two kilometers from where Veronica Knight's remains had been found by Bill and Valda Thomas a year earlier. The chances that two people had innocently lost their lives out in this remote area seemed incredibly unlikely.
马基塔一家的故事成了当地的头条新闻，记者们将之与同期失踪的其他年轻女性联系起来。但没有人提供能阐明这些女孩下落的信息。几周后的1979年4月15日，一名警察正骑着摩托车在斯图尔特公路上巡逻。那是复活节长周末，由于驾车者从城市涌向附近的约克半岛（York Peninsula）和巴罗萨山谷（Barossa Valley）葡萄酒产区度假，北方道路上这段时间历来非常危险。这名警察正在寻找任何鲁莽行为，突然被四名年轻男子拦了下来。他们在特鲁罗附近的灌木丛中猎兔时，发现了一些令人不安的东西。他们把用塑料布包着的东西交给了警察。那是一个人类头点头骨。这群年轻人引导警察来到他们发现头骨的牧场。那是沿着沼泽路，距离篱笆线仅50米。对调查人员来说，这个地点立即敲响了警钟。这个地点距离比尔和瓦尔达·托马斯一年前发现维罗妮卡·奈特遗骸的地方不到两公里。两个人无辜地在这个偏远地区丧生的几率似乎微乎其微。

21
00:22:59.000 --> 00:24:28.000
A full-scale search of the area was ordered and it wasn't long before several more human bones were recovered as well as a pair of women's underwear and a small purse. When news of the discovery hit the media, many anticipated that the body would be identified as Julie Makeda. Married couple Andreas and Margarita Pittman were sitting down to watch the evening news in their home in Taperoo, a suburb 18 kilometers northwest of Adelaide. The house had felt quiet ever since their 16-year-old daughter Sylvia Pittman had vanished just over two years prior. Sylvia had dreams of becoming a model and not wanting to rely on her good looks alone, she had secured a job in a retail boutique in order to learn more about the fashion industry. On Sunday, February 6, 1977, she walked from her home in Taperoo to catch the bus to work on nearby Military Road and was never seen again. When Sylvia failed to return home that night, her parents were immediately worried but weren't sure what to think. Not long before her disappearance, Sylvia had run off to Melbourne where she'd ultimately been placed in a women's detention centre.
当局下令对该地区进行全面搜查，不久之后，又找回了几块人类骨头以及一双女性内衣和一个小钱包。当发现的消息登上媒体头条时，许多人预计这具尸体将被辨认为朱莉·马基塔。已婚夫妇安德烈亚斯（Andreas）和玛格丽塔·皮特曼（Margarita Pittman）正坐在阿德莱德西北18公里处的郊区塔佩罗（Taperoo）的家中看晚间新闻。自从他们16岁的女儿西尔维亚·皮特曼（Sylvia Pittman）两年多前神秘失踪以来，家里一直感到很安静。西尔维亚梦想成为一名模特，并且不想仅仅依仗自己的美貌，她在一家零售精品店找到了一份工作，以便更多地了解时尚产业。1977年2月6日星期日，她从塔佩罗的家中步行去乘公交车，到附近军事路（Military Road）上的工作地点上班，就再也没有露面。当西尔维亚那天晚上没有回家时，她的父母立刻担心起来，但又不知该作何感想。就在她失踪前不久，西尔维亚曾离家出走去墨尔本，最终被安置在女子拘留中心。

22
00:24:28.000 --> 00:25:51.000
The whole experience had frightened her and she vowed to her parents that she'd never run away again. To avoid a recurrence, Andreas and Margarita had made an effort to give Sylvia more freedom and they didn't think she'd betray their trust for a second time. They had filed a missing person report but remained uncertain as to whether Sylvia had gone off on her own accord or fallen victim to foul play. Now watching the news, they saw that skeletal remains of an unidentified female had been found in a paddock near Truro for the second time in almost a year. An item of jewellery had also been recovered, a 14 carat gold necklace featuring a medallion of a guardian angel. Andreas and Margarita's hearts sank. There was no question that the necklace belonged to Sylvia. It had been given to her by her Austrian grandmother and she treasured it dearly. The Pittman's contacted police who used the dental records to confirm that the second body found near Truro was indeed that of Sylvia Pittman.
这段经历吓坏了她，她向父母发誓绝不再离家出走。为了避免重蹈覆辙，安德烈亚斯和玛格丽塔努力给西尔维亚更多的自由，他们不认为她会第二次辜负他们的信任。他们报了人口失踪案，但仍然不确定西尔维亚是自愿出走还是成了暴力犯罪的受害者。现在看着新闻，他们看到在特鲁罗附近的牧场上，近一年来第二次发现了未识别女性的骨骼残骸。还找回了一件首饰，一条14K金项链，上面挂着一个守护天使的奖章。安德烈亚斯和玛格丽塔的心沉了下去。毫无疑问，这条项链属于西尔维亚。那是她奥地利祖母送给她的，她非常珍视。皮特曼夫妇联系了警方，警方利用牙科记录确认特鲁罗附近发现的第二具尸体确实是西尔维亚·皮特曼。

23
00:25:51.000 --> 00:27:13.000
Any remaining doubts the police may have had were immediately quashed. It was now clear they were dealing with a serial killer. A search party was quickly amassed and 85 police officers and cadets set up camp in the bushland surrounding Truro. With a designated search area of 20 square kilometres, they worked around the clock, sleeping in tents and makeshift bunks inside horse floats. Cadets crawled through the rugged terrain on hands and knees while officers covered the area on horseback and motorcycle. They persevered through unseasonable heat and light rain, convinced that more discoveries were imminent. Foraging near the base of a tree near where Veronica's body was found, police came across a woman's shoe. It matched the description of one that was worn by another young woman who had gone missing around the same time as Veronica and Sylvia. Police had their suspicions as to who the shoe belonged to but awaited the discovery of any remains before making an announcement.
警方可能存有的任何剩余疑虑立即被消除了。现在很清楚，他们正在应对一个连环杀手。搜救队迅速集结，85名警察和学员在特鲁罗周边的灌木丛中搭建了营地。划定的搜索面积达20平方公里，他们夜以继日地工作，睡在帐篷和马匹拖车内的简易床铺上。学员们手脚并用地爬过崎岖的地形，而警官们则骑着马匹和摩托车巡视该地区。他们顶住了反常的高温和小雨，坚信会有更多的发现。在靠近维罗妮卡尸体发现地的一棵树根附近搜寻时，警察偶然发现了一只女鞋。它与另一名年轻女性穿的一只鞋的描述相符，该女子在维罗妮卡和西尔维亚失踪前后失踪。警方对这只鞋属于谁心存怀疑，但在宣布之前等待任何遗骸的发现。

24
00:27:13.000 --> 00:28:27.000
Then, on April 26, 1979, a team of mounted police carefully led their horses over a barbed wire fence bordering Swamp Road. They foraged around and spotted something under a large branch. It was a shallow grave. Crudely covered by light bush was the skeleton of 15-year-old Connie Yordanides. Connie had been having some ongoing conflict at home. The teenager sought a level of freedom that her strict Greek parents didn't allow and in turn they constantly butted heads. Following the advice of some school friends, Connie reached out to the Department of Community Welfare and lodged a complaint against her parents. She was temporarily placed in a foster home before the Department negotiated some conditions for her return to the family home. Connie's parents reluctantly agreed with the Department's recommendation that they should let her come and go from the house as she pleased.
然后，在1979年4月26日，一队骑警小心翼翼地引导着他们的马匹越过沼泽路旁边的带刺铁丝网篱笆。他们在四处搜寻时，在大树枝下发现了一些东西。那是一个浅浅的坟墓。粗糙地被浅矮树丛覆盖着的是15岁女孩康妮·约达尼季斯（Connie Yordanides）的骨骼。康妮家里最近一直有一些持续的冲突。这个十几岁的女孩追求一种她严厉的希腊父母不允许的自由度，因此他们经常顶撞。在一些学校朋友的建议下，康妮联系了社区福利部，并对父母提出了投诉。在部门协商她返回家庭的一些条件之前，她被暂时安置在一个寄宿家庭中。康妮的父母不情愿地同意了福利部的建议，即应该让她随心所欲地进出家门。

25
00:28:27.000 --> 00:29:53.000
It pained them to do so but they were just happy to have their daughter back home. At around 6pm on February 9, 1977, Connie had told her parents she was going to her boyfriend's house and then to a drive-in movie. Her boyfriend lived one kilometer from her home in the western suburb of Brooklyn Park and Connie planned to walk there. Her father offered to drive her but Connie declined. When she failed to return home that night, her parents thought she had simply run away. They made inquiries with Connie's friends and contacted the Department of Community Welfare to voice their concerns but four days passed before they reported Connie's disappearance to the police. Detective Sergeant Bob Giles had come across Connie's missing person file when piecing together the pattern of mysterious disappearances during the summer of 1977 and was convinced that she had fallen victim to foul play. Investigators came to the conclusion she may have lied to her parents about going to the drive-in and had instead headed into the city where she crossed paths with the killer.
这样做让他们感到痛苦，但只要女儿能回家，他们就很高兴了。1977年2月9日晚上6点左右，康妮告诉父母她要去男朋友家，然后去看汽车电影院。她的男朋友住在西部郊区布鲁克林公园（Brooklyn Park）她家一公里外的地方，康妮计划步行去那里。她父亲提出开车送她，但康妮拒绝了。当她那天晚上没有回家时，她的父母以为她只是离家出走了。他们向康妮的朋友们打听，并联系社区福利部表达他们的担忧，但过了四天他们才向警方报告康妮失踪。侦探警长鲍勃·吉尔斯在拼凑1977年夏天神秘失踪案的模式时，偶然遇到了康妮的失踪档案，并坚信她成了暴力犯罪的受害者。调查人员得出结论，她可能对父母撒谎说要去汽车电影院，而是去了市中心，在那里她与凶手狭路相逢。

26
00:29:53.000 --> 00:31:20.000
When the third body found near Truro was confirmed to be Connie, her father Yordana partly blamed the Department of Community Welfare for his daughter's death. Had they not interfered with his family business, Yordana said he never would have let Connie go out on her own the night she vanished. He told reporters, I strongly believe it and that's what counts at the moment. Just 100 meters from where Connie's body was found, the same team of mounted police came across the remains of another young woman. Her platform sandal still perfectly intact. The victim was slightly older than the others, giving police an indication as to who it might be. 26-year-old Vicki May Howe worked as a nurse's aide. She was a divorced mother of three who lived with her boyfriend Harry in the western suburb of Corralta Park. At around 7pm on February 9, 1977, Vicki was washing the dishes after dinner when she overheard a conversation between Harry and his brother. She thought they were talking about her and became upset.
当特鲁罗附近发现的第三具尸体被确认是康妮时，她的父亲约尔达纳（Yordana）部分将女儿的死归咎于社区福利部。约尔达纳说，如果他们没有干涉他的家庭事务，他绝不会让康妮在她失踪的那天晚上独自外出。他告诉记者，我坚信这一点，这才是目前最重要的。就在发现康妮尸体100米远的地方，同一支骑警队偶然发现了另一名年轻女性的遗骸。她的厚底凉鞋仍然完好无损。受害者比其他人稍大一些，给警方提供了一个线索，让他们知道她可能是谁。26岁的维琪·梅·豪（Vicki May Howe）是一名护士助理。她是一位育有三个孩子的离异母亲，与男友哈里（Harry）住在西部郊区的科拉尔塔公园（Corralta Park）。1977年2月9日晚上7点左右，维琪在晚饭后洗碗时，无意中听到哈里和他兄弟的对话。她以为他们在谈论她，因而感到心烦意乱。

27
00:31:20.000 --> 00:32:40.000
Vicki told Harry she was going outside to get some clothes off the line, but instead she walked outside onto Anzac Highway, a main thoroughfare connecting Adelaide CBD with Glenelg Beach. She was last seen waiting for a city-bound bus. Vicki had run off in the past, so Harry didn't think too much of it at first and was convinced she'd come home eventually. When Vicki still hadn't returned four days later, Harry told Vicki's sister, who reported her as missing. Detectives had an identified Vicki as one of the potential victims of the Truro serial killer. She was older than the others and didn't quite fit the same profile. However, after forensic investigators examined the fourth body found in the bush, there was no doubt that it was Vicki Howe. In addition to Julie Makeda, there were still two more women who the task force believed had fallen victim to the same fate. 15-year-old Tanya Kenny was a bright student who naturally excelled at school, but despite her academic achievements, she often clashed with her parents.
维琪告诉哈里她要出去把晾衣绳上的衣服收进来，但她却走到了安扎克公路（Anzac Highway）上，这是一条连接阿德莱德中央商务区与格雷尔格海滩（Glenelg Beach）的主要干道。她最后一次被看到是在等一辆开往市中心的公交车。维琪过去曾离家出走过，所以哈里起初并没有多想，并坚信她最终会回家。当四天后维琪依然没有回来时，哈里告诉了维琪的姐姐，后者报了人口失踪。侦探们已将维琪确定为特鲁罗连环杀手的潜在受害者之一。她比其他人年长，不太符合相同的档案。然而，在法医调查人员检查了在灌木丛中发现的第四具尸体后，毫无疑问这就是维琪·豪。除了朱莉·马基塔，专案组还认为有两名女性落入了同样的命运。15岁的塔尼娅·肯尼（Tanya Kenny）是一个聪明的学生，在学校里自然表现优异，但尽管她学业优异，她却经常与父母发生冲突。

28
00:32:40.000 --> 00:33:56.000
Tanya desired a level of freedom that her parents wouldn't allow, and this led to some ongoing problems. On December 31, 1976, Tanya had asked her parents if she could attend a New Year's Eve party at the Cliton Rock Surf Life Saving Club in Haberah, a coastal town 80km south of Adelaide. Her parents said no, but Tanya went anyway. She spent New Year's Day there too, before leaving to catch a bus back to Adelaide on January 2, 1977. Nobody had seen or heard from Tanya since, and her parents expressly forbid the police from seeking any publicity about her disappearance. The seventh and final woman that police believed could be a victim of the Truro serial killer was 20-year-old Deborah Lamb. Deborah was considered a loner who could be a little naive. In mid-1976, she gave birth to a daughter she felt she was unable to care for and had given up for adoption at six months old.
塔尼娅渴望父母不允许的自由度，这导致了一些持续的问题。1976年12月31日，塔尼娅问父母她是否可以参加位于哈贝拉（Haberah）的克利夫顿洛克冲浪救生俱乐部（Cliton Rock Surf Life Saving Club）的除夕派对，这是一个距离阿德莱德以南80公里的沿海小镇。她的父母说不行，但塔尼娅还是去了。她也在那里度过了元旦，然后于1977年1月2日离开，搭乘公交车返回阿德莱德。自那以后，没有人见过或听说过塔尼娅，她的父母明确禁止警方就她的失踪寻求任何公开报道。警方认为可能是特鲁罗连环杀手受害者的第七个也是最后一个女性是20岁的黛博拉·兰姆（Deborah Lamb）。黛博拉被认为是一个有点天真的独行侠。1976年中期，她生下一个女儿，觉得自己无力抚养，并在六个月大时将其送养。

29
00:33:56.000 --> 00:34:59.000
By February 12, 1977, Deborah was living in a caravan park in the north-eastern suburb of Windsor Gardens. At around 6pm, she boarded a bus into the city for a night out and never returned. Deborah hadn't spoken to her family for five months, but she was noticed to be missing when she failed to claim her unemployment benefits, or pick up some items of clothing that she had waiting for her at a church drop-in centre. Her bank account also hadn't been touched since the night of her disappearance. Investigators were convinced that the bodies of Julie Makeda, Tanya Kenney and Deborah Lamb were amongst those dumped in the bushland surrounding Truro, and the search for their remains continued for days. However, no further discoveries were made, and the search camp eventually disbanded,
到1977年2月12日，黛博拉住在东北郊温莎花园（Windsor Gardens）的一个房车公园里。晚上6点左右，她登上一辆去市里过夜的公交车，再也没有回来。黛博拉已经五个月没有和家人说话了，但当她未能领取失业救济金，或者没有去教会收容中心取她等候在那里的几件衣物时，人们注意到她失踪了。自她失踪的那天晚上起，她的银行账户也一直没有动过。调查人员确信朱莉·马基塔、塔尼娅·肯尼和黛博拉·兰姆的尸体就在被倾倒在特鲁罗周围灌木丛中的尸体之列，对她们遗骸的搜寻持续了数天。然而，没有更多的发现，搜寻营地最终解散了，

30
00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:51.000
with investigators instead turning their focus on catching the killer. The case quickly made national headlines, with the area described as a, quote, graveyard of missing girls. Members of the small Truro community were outraged that their quiet, safe town had become associated with such a grisly murder investigation. They firmly believed the killer wasn't from the area. If he was a local, he would know about the nearby mineshafts and would have dumped the bodies there instead, where they'd likely never be found. The community also resented the name of their town being tied to the crimes.
调查人员转而将重点放在抓捕凶手上。该案迅速登上全国头条，该地区被描述为一个引号中的“失踪女孩墓地”。小镇特鲁罗的居民对他们安静、安全的城镇与如此可怕的谋杀案调查产生联系感到愤怒。他们坚信凶手不是本地人。如果他是本地人，他会知道附近的矿井，并会把尸体倒在那里，在那里它们很可能永远不会被找到。社区还对他们城镇的名字与这些罪行联系在一起感到不满。

31
00:35:51.000 --> 00:36:44.000
There were other townships closer to the dumping ground, but the media felt that Truro was more recognisable to the greater public. While the people of Truro agonised, residents of Adelaide seemed oddly indifferent. Despite there being four confirmed murder victims and three more missing women suspected of having fallen to the same fate, there was an astonishing lack of public outrage. The only witness to any of the alleged abductions had been Julie Makeda's friend Max, who had seen a man in a white car approach Julie on the night that she vanished. Partly because of this, people seemed to accept that the young women had likely accepted a lift with a stranger and therefore only had themselves to blame.
还有其他距离抛尸地点更近的小镇，但媒体认为特鲁罗对广大公众来说更容易辨认。当特鲁罗的人民痛苦煎熬时，阿德莱德的居民似乎表现得奇怪地冷漠。尽管有四名确诊的谋杀受害者和另外三名涉嫌遭遇相同命运的失踪女性，但公众的愤怒却惊人地缺乏。任何所谓绑架事件的唯一目击者是朱莉·马基塔的朋友马克斯，他在朱莉失踪的那天晚上看到一个开着白车的人接近朱莉。部分由于这一点，人们似乎接受了这些年轻女性可能接受了陌生人的搭便车，因此只能怪她们自己。

32
00:36:44.000 --> 00:37:31.000
One journalist noted, Outrage and a hue and cry for the killer are noticeably absent. An article in the Sydney Morning Herald read, Adelaide seems to have accepted their deaths as if they are merely additions to the road toll, an abnormal willingness to treat them as an inevitable part of modern life. There were other reasons that public fears were subdued. For one, the distance between Adelaide and the dumping ground created a false sense of safety for those who lived in the city and its surrounding suburbs. Secondly, almost two years had passed since the killer last struck,
一位记者指出，对凶手的愤怒和呐喊明显缺失。悉尼先驱晨报的一篇文章写道，阿德莱德似乎已经接受了她们的死亡，仿佛它们只是交通事故死亡人数的增加，这种对将它们视为现代生活不可避免的一部分的异常意愿。公众恐惧得到平息还有其他原因。一方面，阿德莱德与抛尸地之间的距离为那些住在城市及周边郊区的人创造了一种虚假的安全感。其次，距离凶手上次行凶已经过去了近两年，

33
00:37:31.000 --> 00:38:38.000
so the majority simply didn't view the serial killer as a looming threat. Given the skeletal state of the remains, there was also no forensic evidence to indicate how any of the young women had died. As one psychologist explained, this fact also hindered emotional reaction. He stated the remains were, quote, Just bones and rotten material, not young girls we can relate to. Undeterred by the lackluster public response, investigators worked tirelessly to solve the case. They reassessed more than 10,000 files of people who had gone missing from South Australia from 1975 onwards to see if any of them fit the same pattern as the slain women. They reexamined reports of attempted abductions that occurred during late 1976 and early 1977 and found they had a number of reports on file.
因此大多数人根本不认为连环杀手是一个逼近的威胁。鉴于遗骸的骨骼状态，也没有法医证据表明这些年轻女性是如何死亡的。正如一位心理学家所解释的那样，这一事实也阻碍了情感反应。他指出，这些遗骸是，引号，只是骨头和腐烂的物质，而不是我们可以产生共鸣的年轻女孩。在对公众冷淡反应毫不气馁的情况下，调查人员夜以继日地努力破案。他们重新评估了1975年以来南澳大利亚失踪人员的10,000多份档案，看看其中是否有符合被杀女性相同模式的人。他们重新审查了1976年末至1977年初发生的企图绑架报告，并发现他们手头有许多此类报告。

34
00:38:38.000 --> 00:40:01.000
Anyone who had witnessed an attempted abduction during this time was asked to come forward and a reward of $30,000 for information that led to an arrest was announced. Anyone with information who wasn't directly involved with the murders was promised a pardon, but despite the reward being a significant amount of money for the era, nobody came forward. At the time, criminal profiling was in its infancy and this new technique was applied to try and pinpoint the likely traits of the Truro killer. A criminologist deduced that the perpetrator likely lived in the inner suburbs of Adelaide and came across as a normal individual who was indistinguishable from anyone else in the community. His first murder had likely stemmed from a sexual encounter with a woman that had gone awry and after realising he could get away with it with no public outcry, he gained the confidence to do it again. He kept returning to Truro to dump the bodies because he felt confident that they wouldn't be found. He might have been a lonely individual and interpreted his victims as troubled or unlikely to be missed.
在此期间目击过企图绑架案的任何人被要求站出来，并宣布悬赏3万澳元以获取能够导致逮捕的信息。向任何未直接参与谋杀但掌握信息的知情者承诺免于起诉，但尽管这笔赏金对那个时代来说是一笔可观的巨款，却没有人站出来。当时，犯罪侧写还处于起步阶段，这种新技术被应用于试图查明特鲁罗杀手的可能特征。一位犯罪学家推测，作案者很可能是阿德莱德内城郊区的居民，表现得像个正常人，与社区中的任何人没有区别。他的第一起谋杀很可能源于与一个女性的性接触出了岔子，在意识到自己可以逃脱且没有引起公众抗议后，他获得了再次作案的自信。他不断回到特鲁罗抛尸，因为他确信尸体不会被找到。他可能是一个孤独的人，并将他的受害者视为有麻烦或不太可能被怀念的人。

35
00:40:01.000 --> 00:41:05.000
One of the biggest questions was why the killer had stopped. His crime spree had taken place in a short period of time with a suspected total of seven women abducted and murdered within just a seven week time frame. Veronica Knight and Tanya Kenney had gone missing one week apart whereas Sylvia Pittman, Connie Jordanides and Vicki Howell vanished within just days of one another. This meant the killer had escalated at a rapid pace. Deborah Lamb then disappeared three days later and after that there were no more suspected victims. So why stop so suddenly? Criminologists believed the killer had either died or been arrested on an unrelated charge and incarcerated. They looked into criminals with a record of violent offending, hoping to identify anyone who fit the profile.
最大的谜团之一是凶手为什么会收手。他的犯罪狂潮发生在很短的一段时间内，据信在短短七周内共有七名女性被绑架和谋杀。维罗妮卡·奈特（Veronica Knight）和塔尼娅·肯尼（Tanya Kenney）是在一周之内相继失踪的，而西尔维娅·皮特曼（Sylvia Pittman）、康妮·乔丹尼德斯（Connie Jordanides）和薇姬·豪厄尔（Vicki Howell）则在几天之内相继消失。这意味着凶手的犯罪升级速度极快。三天后，黛博拉·兰姆（Deborah Lamb）也失踪了，此后便再也没有疑似受害者。那么，为什么要突然收手呢？犯罪学家认为，凶手要么已经死亡，要么因另一项罪名被捕并收监。他们调查了有暴力犯罪记录的罪犯，希望能找出符合这一特征的人。

36
00:41:05.000 --> 00:42:09.000
Desperate for answers, the reward for information was increased to $40,000. This time it was enough to encourage someone to speak. 32 year old James Miller was exercising in the remand yard of Adelaide Jail when he looked over and caught sight of a young man he had never seen before. To Miller, the young inmate didn't look like a typical criminal. He had a clean look with no tattoos and seemed to take pride in his appearance. Miller, who had weathered skin and was slightly balding, was instantly struck by the young man's good looks. Miller himself was no stranger to institutions. Born Melville Raymond Juss, before later changing his name via Deedpaal, he was raised in a poor family of six children in Adelaide's West.
由于急需答案，提供线索的悬赏金被提高到了4万美元。这一次，悬赏金额终于足够让人开口了。32岁的詹姆斯·米勒（James Miller）正在阿德莱德监狱（Adelaide Jail）的还押操场上锻炼身体，这时他抬头瞥见了一个从未见过的年轻男子。在米勒看来，这个年轻的囚犯看起来不像个典型的罪犯。他面容干净，没有纹身，似乎很注重仪容。米勒饱经风霜、略微秃顶，当即被这个年轻人的美貌所吸引。米勒本人也是个监狱常客。他出生时名叫梅尔维尔·雷蒙德·朱斯（Melville Raymond Juss），后来通过变更姓名契约（Deedpoll）改了名，在阿德莱德西部的一个有六个孩子的贫困家庭中长大。

37
00:42:09.000 --> 00:43:13.000
He began stealing at a young age and by 11 he was sent to live in a reformatory in the eastern suburbs. By 15 he fled the reformatory, stole a car and drove it into state. He was eventually caught and sentenced to hard labor in New South Wales's Long Bay Jail. From that point on, James Miller was in and out of jail for a range of non-violent offences including theft, larceny and breaking and entering. He didn't have any close friends and considered himself to be a lone wolf. Miller was gay and when he caught sight of the young new inmate across the remand yard, he felt an immediate attraction. The inmate appeared to be in his late teens and was about five foot seven inches tall with shoulder length shiny black hair and a warm, friendly smile. The inmate introduced himself as 20 year old Christopher Robin Warrell.
他很小就开始偷窃，11岁就被送去东郊的一家教养所。15岁时，他逃离教养所，偷了一辆汽车并一路开出了州。他最终被抓获，并在新南威尔士州的朗贝i监狱（Long Bay Jail）被判处苦役。从那时起，詹姆斯·米勒因盗窃、偷盗和入室盗窃等一系列非暴力犯罪而频繁进出监狱。他没有什么亲密的朋友，认为自己是一匹孤狼。米勒是个同性恋，当他在还押操场上瞥见那个年轻的新囚犯时，他顿时产生了一种吸引力。这个囚犯看起来二十岁出头，身高约五英尺七英寸，留着齐肩的亮黑色头发，带着温暖友善的微笑。这名囚犯自我介绍说，他是20岁的克里斯托弗·罗宾·沃雷尔（Christopher Robin Warrell）。

38
00:43:13.000 --> 00:44:19.000
Warrell had been raised by his grandmother and left high school to serve three years in the Royal Australian Air Force. He was discharged after being deemed incompatible with service life. This was his first time in jail, although he had a prior suspended sentence for armed robbery after he had robbed a hitchhiker of $1.50. This time he'd been accused of rape, a crime which he staunchly denied. Chris Warrell explained to Miller that he'd been drunk driving when he picked up a female hitchhiker and the two had engaged in sex. Afterwards, the woman accused him of raping her, but Warrell was too intoxicated to remember any of the details. Miller immediately believed Warrell when he said he was innocent. He was simply too good looking and too charming to have to resort to rape.
沃雷尔由祖母抚养长大，高中辍学后在澳大利亚皇家空军服役三年。因被认为不适合军旅生活，他被勒令退伍。这是他第一次入狱，尽管他此前曾因抢劫一名搭便车者1.5美元而被判武装抢劫缓刑。这一次，他被指控强奸，但他坚决否认这一罪名。克里斯·沃雷尔向米勒解释说，他在酒后驾车时搭载了一名女性搭便车者，两人发生了性关系。事后，那名女子指控他强奸，但沃雷尔当时醉得不省人事，根本记不清任何细节。米勒立刻相信了沃雷尔的无辜。他觉得对方长得太帅、太有魅力了，根本不需要沦落到去强奸。

39
00:44:19.000 --> 00:45:15.000
Miller and Warrell began chatting and over the next few days a friendship bloomed. They were an unlikely pair, Warrell was confident and arrogant, whereas Miller was self-conscious and submissive, but their opposing personalities meshed perfectly. Within a week, they had requested to move into a cell together. From that point on, the two men spent all their time together. They decided to solidify their bond by becoming blood brothers. A ritual that involved them each slicing their own wrist and then placing their two bleeding wounds together. One night, out of nowhere, Warrell turned to Miller and said, I am a psychopathic killer. Miller wasn't sure what to make of the comet.
米勒和沃雷尔开始聊天，在接下来的几天里，一段友谊迅速萌芽。他们是一对很不相配的组合：沃雷尔自信而傲慢，而米勒则自卑且顺从，但他们截然不到的性格却完美地契合在了一起。一周之内，他们就申请搬进同一个牢房。从那时起，这两个人把所有时间都泡在了一起。他们决定通过成为血盟兄弟来巩固彼此的纽带。这是一个仪式，两人各自割破自己的手腕，然后将两个流血的伤口贴在一起。一天晚上，沃雷尔毫无征兆地转向米勒说：“我是一个精神病杀手。”米勒不知道该怎么看待这句话。

40
00:45:15.000 --> 00:46:25.000
Was Warrell being serious or just trying to scare him? He brushed it off, but Warrell's words stayed with him. Warrell was eventually found guilty of the rape and sentenced to six years in Yatla prison. Miller was released shortly after, but it wasn't long before he was arrested again, this time for stealing more than 4,000 pairs of sunglasses. He was sentenced to 18 months in Yatla and was relieved to be reunited with his blood brother. The pair's bond continued to strengthen and they began making plans to live together on the outside. On February 11, 1976, James Miller was finally released, but as an ex-con, he wasn't allowed to visit the jail until he'd been free for a year. To skirt around this rule, he began disguising himself as a woman in order to visit Warrell.
沃雷尔是认真的，还是只是想吓唬他？他把这话抛在了脑后，但沃雷尔的话一直萦绕在他心头。沃雷尔最终被裁定强奸罪名成立，在亚特拉监狱（Yatla prison）被判处六年监禁。米勒不久后获释，但没过多久他又被捕了，这一次是因为偷窃了4000多副太阳镜。他在亚特拉监狱被判处18个月监禁，并庆幸能与他的血兄弟团聚。两人的纽带不断加深，他们开始计划出狱后一起生活。1976年2月11日，詹姆斯·米勒终于刑满释放，但作为一名刑满释放人员，他必须在获得自由一年后才能探监。为了钻这个规则的空子，他开始把自己打扮成女人去探望沃雷尔。

41
00:46:25.000 --> 00:47:25.000
17 months into Warrell's sentence, he applied for parole. During his time behind bars, the only rule he had broken was owning a forbidden gold earring. Otherwise, he'd been a model prisoner. Warrell's parole officer visited his mother and stepfather to ask if they were prepared to accept Warrell back into their home. They weren't, saying they were worried that he'd be a bad influence on his younger brother. Partly because of this, his application for parole was denied. In June of 1976, Warrell applied for parole for the second time and this time his application was successful. After serving just two years of his six-year sentence, Warrell was released at the age of 23.
在沃雷尔服刑17个月后，他申请了假释。在铁窗生涯中，他违反的唯一规矩就是拥有一只违禁的金耳环。除此之外，他一直是个模范囚犯。沃雷尔的假释官拜访了他的母亲和继父，询问他们是否准备好接受沃雷尔回家。他们拒绝了，称他们担心沃雷尔会对他的弟弟产生不良影响。部分由于这个原因，他的假释申请被驳回了。1976年6月，沃雷尔第二次申请假释，这一次他的申请获批了。在服完六年刑期的两年后，沃雷尔在23岁时获释了。

42
00:47:25.000 --> 00:48:32.000
The two cellmates were reunited on the outside at last. They spent every day together, sleeping at Miller's sister's house or at the homes of other friends while they tried to get enough money together for their own flat. They borrowed cars from friends and co-workers whenever they could while saving up to buy a car of their own. Both men got a job doing outdoor work for the council and they spent their spare time cruising the streets of Adelaide, frequenting the local pubs and picking up women. Chris Warrell had an appealing charm and confidence about him and coupled with his boyish good looks, women were naturally attracted to him. He had no trouble approaching women on the street and convincing them to come for a ride with him. If they ever seemed wary about Miller's presence, Warrell would tell them that Miller was his father or uncle, which put them at ease.
两个狱友终于在外面重聚了。他们每天都在一起，睡在米勒姐姐家或其他朋友家里，同时努力攒钱买一套属于他们自己的公寓。他们一有机会就向朋友和同事借车，同时攒钱买一辆属于自己的车。两人都在议会找到了一份户外清洁的工作，闲暇时他们会开着车在阿德莱德的街道上兜风，常去当地的酒吧搭讪女性。克里斯·沃雷尔有一种吸引人的魅力和自信，再加上他那充满男孩气的英俊外表，女性自然会被他吸引。他在街上搭讪女性并说服她们搭他的顺风车毫不费力。如果她们对米勒的存在感到警惕，沃雷尔就会告诉她们米勒是他的父亲或叔叔，这让她们打消了顾虑。

43
